# Domain where BOTH original and replication studies primarily used students

## Final hypothesis (answer)
The **Social** domain (one of Social / Cognitive / Economics) is the one in which BOTH the
original and the replication studies predominantly used student subjects. Among Social pairs,
62/74 (about 83.8%) original studies used student subjects and 60/74 (about 81.1%)
replications used student subjects — the values closest to the quoted 82.4% (original) and
80.9% (replication). Student-subject dominance holds under every pooling variant, while
Cognitive falls well short on the original side and Economics exceeds both targets.

## Evidence (per-domain student-subject shares)

Primary — pair-level (141 unique original/replication pairs):
| domain    | n pairs | original used students | replication used students |
|-----------|---------|------------------------|---------------------------|
| Social    | 74      | 62/74 = **83.78%**    | 60/74 = **81.08%**        |
| Cognitive | 49      | 37/49 = 75.51%        | 40/49 = 81.63%            |
| Economics | 18      | 17/18 = 94.44%        | 18/18 = 100.00%           |

Sensitivity — row-level (927 rows, per-lab weighting) and per-lab (786 rows):
| domain    | row-level orig | row-level repl | per-lab orig | per-lab repl |
|-----------|----------------|----------------|--------------|--------------|
| Social    | 548/668=82.04%| 524/668=78.44%| 486/594=81.82%| 464/594=78.11%|
| Cognitive | 172/241=71.37%| 202/241=83.82%| 135/192=70.31%| 162/192=84.38%|
| Economics | 17/18=94.44%  | 18/18=100%    | (no per-lab rows)         |

Social is the only domain that simultaneously approximates BOTH quoted values across all
three pooling variants. Cognitive's original-student share (~70-76%) is far below 82.4%;
Economics (94.4%/100%) exceeds both.

## Caveat (workflow note, not part of the answer)
The exact decimals 82.4/80.9 are not reproduced to the tenth by this CSV; closest are the
pair-level Social values (83.8%/81.1%) and row-level values (82.0%/78.4%). The quoted
decimals most likely come from the source paper's descriptive table under a slightly
different study filter. The robust, consistent qualitative identification (Social) is what
the question asks for.

## Pipeline
- Files: data/meta-regression_study_data_heterogeneity_in_replication_projects.csv (927x57;
  contains discipline, subjects.o, subjects.r, id, project, aggregated, drop, lab_id).
  replication_success file (143x15) has NO discipline/subjects columns and is not usable here.
- Units: id = original/replication pair (141 unique: rpp 97, ee 18, ml1 16, ml3 10).
  subjects.o constant within every pair; subjects.r varies per lab for ml1/ml3.
- Primary computation: pair-level (representative row per id); sensitivity: row-level (927)
  and per-lab (786). NA pair (rpp.154, Social) counted as not-student; `drop` flag not used
  for exclusion (documented in script analysis_subjects_by_discipline.py).
